<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1773–1919</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Province of West Prussia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was a province of the Kingdom of Prussia formed from most of Royal Prussia, which Prussia gained from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition</a> of 1772; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> followed in 1793. Its capital was Danzig, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> most of the province went to Poland under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-prussia.jpg" alt="West Prussia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Provinz_Westpreu%C3%9Fen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
